tempts and leveraging intuitive, domain-specific insights for feature creation:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Relative difficulty per line of code\npython\n# difficulty_per_loc\n# Ratio of Halstead difficulty (d) to lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the complexity of the logic per line of code. Higher values may indicate intricate code prone to defects.\ndf['difficulty_per_loc'] = df['d'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Ratio of unique operands to total operands\npython\n# uniq_operand_ratio\n# Ratio of unique operands (uniq_Opnd) to total operands (total_Opnd).\n# Usefulness: This feature measures lexical diversity in the codebase, where a low ratio could suggest repetition or redundant logic, potentially correlating with defects.\ndf['uniq_operand_ratio'] = df['uniq_Opnd'] / (df['total_Opnd'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Halstead effort per unit complexity \npython\n# effort_per_complexity\n# Ratio of Halstead effort (e) to cyclomatic complexity (v(g)).\n# Usefulness: This feature estimates the effort required per unit of structural complexity in the module. Higher values might indicate poorly written or overly complex code, increasing defect risk.\ndf['effort_per_complexity'] = df['e'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns: Removing redundancy and low-impact features\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'total_Opnd' is dropped\n# 'total_Opnd' is dropped because its information is captured in the newly created 'uniq_operand_ratio' feature, which provides normalized insight.\ndf.drop(columns=['total_Opnd'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column 'd' is dropped\n# 'd' (Halstead difficulty) is dropped because its relationship with 'loc' is better represented in the 'difficulty_per_loc' feature.\ndf.drop(columns=['d'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column 'e' is dropped\n# 'e' (Halstead effort) is dropped as its contribution is captured in the 'effort_per_complexity' feature.\ndf.drop(columns=['e'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Recap of Proposed Transformations:\n1. difficulty_per_loc: Measures the density of Halstead difficulty relative to module size.\n2. uniq_operand_ratio: Captures the diversity of unique operands in the code.\n3. effort_per_complexity: Quantifies the effort required per unit of software complexity.\n\nBy introducing these features and dropping redundant columns, the model gains stronger, more interpretable features aligned with software defect dynamics.", "python\n# Feature 1: log_loc\n# Log transformation of the number of lines of code (loc), which normalizes skewed data.\n# Usefulness: Since loc is highly skewed with a mean much higher than the median, a log transformation helps compress the range and may improve the model's performance on features with non-linear relationships to defects.\ndf['log_loc'] = np.log(df['loc'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: operator_to_operand_ratio\n# Ratio of total operators to total operands (total_Op / total_Opnd).\n# Usefulness: This ratio provides a measure of how effectively operands (e.g., variables) are being used in conjunction with operators. A higher or lower ratio may correlate with inefficient or overly complex code, which could lead to increased defect probability.\ndf['operator_to_operand_ratio'] = df['total_Op'] / (df['total_Opnd'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: halstead_difficulty_length_ratio\n# Ratio of Halstead difficulty (d) to Halstead program length (l).\n# Usefulness: This feature captures the relative challenge of writing or understanding the program code in terms of its length and difficulty metric. Modules with high ratios may indicate a higher possibility of defects due to increased complexity relative to the code size.\ndf['halstead_difficulty_length_ratio'] = df['d'] / (df['l'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping columns with redundancy or low information\n# Explanation why the column l is dropped\n# 'l' is dropped as its information is now represented in the new feature 'halstead_difficulty_length_ratio', which normalizes it with another related feature.\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping columns with redundancy or low information\n# Explanation why the column loc is dropped\n# 'loc' is dropped as its distribution has been normalized and captured in the new feature 'log_loc'.\ndf.drop(columns=['loc'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping columns with redundancy or low information\n# Explanation why the column total_Opnd is dropped\n# 'total_Opnd' is dropped as its relationship with 'total_Op' is captured in 'operator_to_operand_ratio'.\ndf.drop(columns=['total_Opnd'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
