ential complexity (ev(g)) and design complexity (iv(g)).\n# Usefulness: Combines two key aspects of complexity into a single feature to capture overall structural complexity in a compact manner, potentially improving the model's ability to recognize complex, defect-prone modules.\ndf['avg_complexity'] = (df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']) / 2\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns: Removing redundancy and irrelevant features\n```python\n# Explanation why the column 'l' is dropped\n# 'l' (Halstead length) is dropped because its relationship is now more effectively captured in 'effort_length_ratio'.\ndf.drop(columns=['l'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column 'ev(g)' is dropped\n# 'ev(g)' (essential complexity) is dropped as its contribution is represented in 'avg_complexity'.\ndf.drop(columns=['ev(g)'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column 'iv(g)' is dropped\n# 'iv(g)' (design complexity) is dropped as its contribution is represented in 'avg_complexity'.\ndf.drop(columns=['iv(g)'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Recap of Proposed Transformations:\n1. **effort_length_ratio**: Normalizes Halstead effort by program length to reflect relative development cost/complexity.\n2. **uniq_opnd_per_loc**: Measures unique operand usage efficiency based on lines of code.\n3. **avg_complexity**: Aggregates essential and design complexity into a unified metric to better capture structural complexity.\n\nBy introducing these features, we aim to highlight relationships within the data, improve interpretability, and enhance defect prediction performance while excluding redundant columns."]
