2026-05-23 18:09:27,019 - INFO - Selected best state: ("Here are the next three steps to generate additional features for improving model performance, following the given dataset characteristics and intuition:\n\n### Step 1: Engineering interaction features related to code complexity\nSince cyclomatic complexity (v(g)), essential complexity (ev(g)), and design complexity (iv(g)) measure different aspects of software complexity, combining them can help create a more generalized complexity contribution for each module.\n\npython\n# total_complexity\n# Combines cyclomatic, essential, and design complexity into a single feature to capture overall complexity of code.\n# Usefulness: Provides a holistic view of complexity, which could improve defect prediction by identifying highly complex modules.\ndf['total_complexity'] = df['v(g)'] + df['ev(g)'] + df['iv(g)']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Signal-to-noise ratio for lines of code and comments\nThe ratio of actual code (lOCode) to the sum of blank lines (lOBlank) and comment lines (lOComment) captures how much of the module is dedicated to meaningful code versus non-functional noise.\n\npython\n# signal_to_noise_loc\n# Measures the density of actual code compared to blank and commented lines, which could reflect coding practice quality.\n# Usefulness: Modules with more meaningful code and fewer comments/blank lines may correlate to fewer defects (or higher developer care).\ndf['signal_to_noise_loc'] = df['lOCode'] / (df['lOComment'] + df['lOBlank'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Defect density proxy through Halstead metrics\nLeverage Halstead metrics such as n (total operators + operands) and b (Halstead bugs indicator) to calculate a defect density proxy, as elevated b per unit size (n) could imply riskier code.\n\npython\n# defect_density\n# Estimates likely defect density in the module based on Halstead bugs (b) normalized by size proxy (n).\n# Usefulness: Helps to identify high-risk areas in the module where potential defects are more prevalent.\ndf['defect_density'] = df['b'] / (df['n'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns: Removing redundant or less informative features\nSome columns may have entirely zero or near-zero variance, rendering them ineffective as model predictors.\n\npython\n# Dropping 'lOComment' and 'locCodeAndComment'\n# 'lOComment': Majority of data points are zero (Q1, Median, Q3 = 0); provides little variation for modeling.\n# 'locCodeAndComment': Mostly zero values for all observations (e.g., Mean = 0.1296, Std = 0.6672).\ndf.drop(columns=['lOComment', 'locCodeAndComment'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n--- \n\n### Recap of Proposed Transformations:\n1. Complexity aggregation: Combine v(g), ev(g), iv(g) into total_complexity.\n2. Signal-to-noise ratio: Ratio of lOCode to non-functional noise quantified by blank lines and comments.\n3. Defect density proxy: Derive b per unit size using defect_density to assess risk. \n\nThese steps aim to add features that combine domain-specific metrics, reduce noise, and target defect patterns, leading to better predictions.", "python\n# Feature 1: loc_per_vg\n# Calculate the ratio of lines of code (loc) per cyclomatic complexity (v(g)).\n# Usefulness: Indicates how distributed the decision points (v(g)) are relative to the size of the module (loc). \n# Higher values may suggest simpler structure per line of code, while lower values may indicate higher complexity density, potentially correlating with defect risk.\ndf['loc_per_vg'] = df['loc'] / (df['v(g)'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: total_Op_per_loc\n# Calculate the ratio of total operators (total_Op) to lines of code (loc).\n# Usefulness: Reflects the prevalence of operations relative to module size. A high ratio might indicate verbose coding or overuse of operators, while a low ratio might suggest concise and efficient coding practices.\ndf['total_Op_per_loc'] = df['total_Op'] / (df['loc'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: halstead_effort_per_size\n# Calculate the ratio of Halstead effort (e) to the total size of the module (total_Op + total_Opnd).\n# Usefulness: Indicates the relative effort required to manage the module size. High values could highlight inefficiencies or overly complex modules linked to higher defect risk.\ndf['halstead_effort_per_size'] = df['e'] / (df['total_Op'] + df['total_Opnd'] + 1e-8)  # Avoid division by zero\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'v(g)' is dropped\n# The cyclomatic complexity 'v(g)' is represented better in 'loc_per_vg', which captures its ratio to the size of the module.\ndf.drop(columns=['v(g)'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'total_Op' is dropped\n# 'total_Op' (total operators) is represented in 'total_Op_per_loc', which provides a richer feature by normalizing it relative to the module size.\ndf.drop(columns=['total_Op'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'e' is dropped\n# The Halstead effort 'e' is captured in the derived feature 'halstead_effort_per_size', which contextualizes effort relative to module size.\ndf.drop(columns=['e'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Recap of Proposed Transformations:\n1. loc_per_vg: Captures complexity density per line of code, enhancing interpretability of structural complexity.\n2. total_Op_per_loc: Highlights operator usage efficiency or verbosity within the module.\n3. halstead_effort_per_size: Relates Halstead effort to total module size, surfacing high effort-to-size modules.\n\nBy adding these new features and dropping redundant columns, we focus on constructing effective, domain-relevant features to improve the defect prediction model while maintaining interpretability."), with improvements -
